      {
        "review_id": 301780556,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r301780556-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Costly and disappointing",
        "text": "I was very disappointed in this site. It's THE most expensive ticket in Kathmandu at a whopping Rs1000. However, there really isn't that much to see. Perhaps you just go for the atmosphere. There are many, many mini temples all over the grounds, some up very steep stairs, some in amongst tranquil gardens, some along the river bank, but they all look very similar and after a short time I had a sense of \"seen one, seen 'em all\". \n\nDown by the river side, you may catch a funeral taking place and it's best not to photograph this activity.\n\nSitting around the temples are holy men in full make up and 'costume', but rather cynically, it appears they really are there just to have their photos taken with tourists and charge for the pleasure. I can't imagine The Archbishop of Canterbury sitting outside Westminster Abbey in his full regalia charging for his photo. \n\nAlas, the most interesting part of the complex is not open to tourists or non-Hindus. A massive, resplendent, golden temple structure sits separately and only believers are permitted to enter. Even if you somehow convince you are a follower, you still are not permitted any photography inside. You can however climb above the compound, up some steep stairs and you can then get a few snaps inside the grounds, but the view is still restricted.\n\nFor the price of this complex, I was disappointed. There are far better and far cheaper options in Kathmandu.",
        "review_tip": null,
